body:
Multiple CI failures across build, lint, and Vercel deploy caused by broken tsconfig path resolution in new plugin packages, missing type annotations, and a React Compiler lint violation.

### tsconfig path resolution (`plugin-workflow`, `plugin-designer`, `plugin-report`)

These packages set `baseUrl: "."` with only `@/*` in `paths`, which clobbered the root tsconfig's `@object-ui/*` workspace path mappings — causing `TS2307: Cannot find module '@object-ui/core'` etc.

Removed `baseUrl`/`paths` (unused `@/` alias) and redundant tsc-specific options (`declaration`, `composite`, `declarationMap`, `noEmit: false`) — these packages build with `vite build` + `vite-plugin-dts`, not `tsc`.

### `@object-ui/react` build

Test files under `src/__tests__/` were included in the `tsc` build scope. Added `"exclude": ["**/*.test.ts", "**/*.test.tsx"]` to match other packages.

### TS7006 implicit `any` annotations

Added explicit types to ~40 callback parameters across:
- `WorkflowDesigner.tsx` — `WorkflowNode`, `WorkflowEdge`, React event types
- `DataModelDesigner.tsx`, `PageDesigner.tsx`, `ReportDesigner.tsx`
- `ReportBuilder.tsx`, `ReportExportEngine.ts`, `ReportViewer.tsx`, `LiveReportExporter.ts`

### `plugin-view` lint error (React Compiler)

`FilterUI.test.tsx` reassigned an outer-scope `let` variable inside a mock component, violating the React Compiler purity rule. Replaced with `React.createContext`/`useContext` to share the `onValueChange` callback between mock `Select` and `SelectItem`:

```tsx
// Before — lint error: "Cannot reassign variables declared outside of the component"
let selectCallback;
const Select = ({ onValueChange }) => { selectCallback = onValueChange; ... };
const SelectItem = ({ value }) => <div onClick={() => selectCallback?.(value)} />;

// After — React Context
const SelectCtx = React.createContext(undefined);
const Select = ({ children, onValueChange }) => (
  <SelectCtx.Provider value={onValueChange}>{children}</SelectCtx.Provider>
);
const SelectItem = ({ value }) => {
  const cb = React.useContext(SelectCtx);
  return <div onClick={() => cb?.(value)} />;
};
```

